he final answer should be 1386.\n\nWait, but in my initial mistaken example, I had different numbers. But now I'm focused on the actual question. So I need to structure this into the template provided.\n\nThe user's example had:\n\n1. Multiply number1 by the ones digit.\n\n2. Multiply number1 by the tens digit (as a ten's place value, like 50).\n\nThen sum the results.\n\nSo applying that to 21 and 66:\n\nStep 1: Multiply 21 by 6 (the ones digit of 66) \u2192 126.\n\nStep 2: Multiply 21 by 60 (the tens digit, which is 6, so 6\u00d710=60) \u2192 1260.\n\nSum: 126 + 1260 = 1386.\n\nTherefore, the answer is 1386. I think that's correct. Let me confirm with another method. Maybe using the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n   21\n\u00d7 66\n-------\n   126  (21 \u00d76)\n+1260   (21 \u00d760)\n-------\n  1386\n\nYes, that's right. Alternatively, 21\u00d766 can be thought of as 21*(60+6) = 21\u00d760 +21\u00d76 = same as above. So all methods confirm that the product is 1386. I think that's solid.\n</think>\n\n# Question:\nWhat is the product of 21 and 66?  \n# Reasoning:  \nLet's think step by step. 66 has two digits, so we can reason in two steps.  \n1. Multiply 21 by the ones place digit (6) of 66:  \n   \\(21 \\times 6 = 126\\).  \n2. Multiply 21 by the tens place digit (60) of 66:  \n   \\(21 \\times 60 = 1260\\).  \nAnswer:  \nNow, sum all the step results: \\(126 + 1260 = 1386\\).  \nSo, the final computed product is \\boxed{1386}.",
