t\'s calculate that: 30 × 90 = 2700.\n\nNow, add the two results: 240 + 2700. That gives 2940.\n\nWait, but let me check with another method to be sure. 30 × 98 can also be thought of as 30 × (100 - 2) = 30×100 - 30×2 = 3000 - 60 = 2940. Yep, that matches. So the answer is 2940.\n\nI need to present this in the template format as per the example. The example had three steps because the second number had three digits. In this case, since it\'s two digits, there should be two steps.\n\nSo the reasoning would be:\n\nLet\'s think step by step. 98 has two digits, so we can do two steps.\n\n1. Multiply 30 by the ones place digit 8 of 98: 30 × 8 = 240.\n\n2. Multiply 30 by the tens place digit 90 of 98: 30 × 90 = 2700.\n\nSum all the step results: 240 + 2700 = 2940.\n\nTherefore, the final product is 2940.\n\nI should make sure there\'s no arithmetic errors here. Let me recheck each step:\n\n30 × 8: 8×30 is 240, correct.\n\n30 × 90: 90 is 9×10, so 30×9 is 270, times 10 is 2700. Correct.\n\nAdding 240 + 2700: 240+2700 = 2940. Yes, correct.\n\nAlternatively, using standard multiplication:\n\n   98\n\n×30\n\n------\n\nMultiply 98 by 0 (units place of 30): 0, but shifted appropriately, but since 30 is 3×10, it\'s easier to do 98×3=294, then ×10 gives 2940. Yep.\n\nAll methods confirm the result is 2940. So the answer is correct.\n\nNow, structuring the answer according to the template provided. The user\'s example had:\n\n# Reasoning:\nLet\'s think step by step. <<number2 153 has three digits, so that we can reason in three steps.>>\n1. <<Multiply number1 27 by the ones place digit 3 of number2 153>>\n2. <<Multiply number1 27 by the tens place digit 50 of number2 153>>\n<<other digits of number2 if it has>>\nAnswer:\nNow, sum all the step results: <<sum of the results>>.\nSo, the final computed product is <<answer>>.\n\nSo applying that to our problem:\n\nThe question is 30 × 98. Number2 (98) has two digits, so two steps.\n\nThe first step is multiplying by the ones digit (8), so 30 ×8=240.\n\nSecond step is multiplying by the tens digit (9, but since it\'s tens place, it\'s 90), so 30×90=2700.\n\nThen sum 240 +2700=2940.\n\nSo the reasoning should be written accordingly.\n\nWait, but in the example\'s reasoning, the second step mentions "the tens place digit 50 of number2 153". So they wrote the digit as 50? Wait, 153\'s tens digit is 5, so they represented it as 50. So in our case, the tens digit of 98 is 9, so it\'s 90. So we should write "the tens place digit 90 of number2 98".\n\nSo the steps would be:\n\n1. Multiply 30 by the ones place digit 8 of 98: 30×8=240.\n\n2. Multiply 30 by the tens place digit 90 of 98: 30×90=2700.\n\nThen sum them to get 2940.\n\nTherefore, the final answer is 2940.\n\nYes, that seems correct. I need to make sure the template is followed exactly as per the example. So the reasoning section should have each step as a bullet point, indicating the place value and the multiplication, then sum them at the end.\n\nAlright, I think that\'s all. Let me put it into the required format now.\n'] 12%|#1        | 59/500 [38:35<3:55:18, 32.01s/it]
