s two digits, so the reasoning would have two steps.\n\nThe user's template example shows:\n\n1. Multiply number1 by the ones place digit (3 of 153)\n\n2. Multiply number1 by the tens place digit (50 of 153)\n\nThen sum those.\n\nSo for 62 × 97:\n\nStep 1: Multiply 62 by the ones digit of 97, which is 7: 62×7=434\n\nStep 2: Multiply 62 by the tens digit of 97, which is 9 (but since it's in the tens place, it's 90): 62×90=5580\n\nSum those: 434 + 5580 = 6014.\n\nYes, exactly. So that's the correct approach following the template. I think that's solid. I don't see any mistakes here. The key steps are breaking down the second number into its digits multiplied by their place values, then multiplying each by the first number and adding.\n\nAnother check: 62 × 97. Let me compute it directly:\n\n62\n\n×97\n\n--------\n\nFirst, 62×7 = 434\n\nThen 62×90 (shift one position to the left):\n\n62×9 = 558, so 5580\n\nAdding 434 + 5580 = 6014. Correct.\n\nAlternatively, using the standard vertical multiplication:\n\n\u2003\u200362\n\n×\xa097\n\n--------\n\n\u2003434 (62×7)\n\n5580 (62×90, shifted one position left)\n\n--------\n\n6014\n\nYep, that's the same result. So confident that's right.\n\nI think that's thorough. The answer is 6014.\n"]
