orrect my earlier mistake. I thought it was 31 and 21, but actually it\'s 57 and 69. My apologies for the confusion.\n\nAlright, starting over with 57 and 69.\n\nThe second number is 69, which has two digits: 9 in the ones place and 6 in the tens place.\n\nSo following the template\'s reasoning steps:\n\n1. Multiply number1 (57) by the ones place digit (9) of number2 (69).\n\nSo 57 * 9. Let\'s compute that:\n\n50*9 = 450\n\n7*9 = 63\n\n450 +63 = 513. So step 1 result is 513.\n\n2. Multiply number1 (57) by the tens place digit (60) of number2 (69).\n\nSo 57 * 60. That can be broken down as 57 *6 *10.\n\n57*6 = 342, so 342*10 = 3420. So step 2 result is 3420.\n\nNow sum the two results: 513 + 3420.\n\nAdding those together:\n\n513 + 3420:\n\n500 + 3400 = 3900\n\n13 + 20 = 33\n\nTotal: 3900 +33 = 3933. Wait, but let\'s do it directly: 513 + 3420 = 3933? Let me check again:\n\n513 + 3420:\n\n3420 + 500 is 3920, plus 13 is 3933. Yes.\n\nWait but 57*69. Let me confirm with standard multiplication:\n\n57\n\nx69\n\n--------\n\nFirst multiply 57 by 9: 513\n\nThen multiply 57 by 60: 3420\n\nAdd them: 513 + 3420 = 3933. Yes, that\'s correct.\n\nSo the answer is 3933.\n\nWait, but let me make sure. Alternatively, 57*70 would be 57*70=3990, so subtract 57 to get 57*(70-1)=3990-57=3933. Yep, that matches.\n\nTherefore, the reasoning steps are correct. The final product is 3933.\n\nSo the template\'s answer section would be filled accordingly.\n\nWait, but the user\'s question says "product of 57 and 69", so I need to present the answer as per the template. The user\'s original template example had the steps split into multiplying by each digit, then summing.\n\nTherefore, in the reasoning section, the steps are:\n\n1. Multiply 57 by 9 (ones place) to get 513.\n\n2. Multiply 57 by 60 (tens place) to get 3420.\n\nThen sum those two results: 513 + 3420 = 3933.\n\nThus, the final answer is 3933.\n\nI think that\'s all. Let me check once more for calculation errors. 57*60 is indeed 34