s digit), resulting in 0. Then multiply 47 by 9 (the tens digit) but shifted left by one, so 47×9 = 423, then shifted becomes 4230. Adding those two results: 0 + 4230 = 4230. So that works.\n\nBut in the example given in the template, when the second number was 153 (three digits), the steps were three, each multiplying by the hundreds, tens, ones. So here, since 90 is two digits, we have two steps.\n\nTherefore, the correct step-by-step would be:\n\n1. Multiply 47 by 0 (ones place) → 0.\n2. Multiply 47 by 90 (the tens place is 9, but since it\'s in the tens place, it\'s 9×10, so actually 47×9×10).\n\nAlternatively, following the structure of the example:\n\n1. Multiply number1 47 by the ones place digit 0 of number2 90 → 47×0 = 0.\n2. Multiply number1 47 by the tens place digit 9 (but since it\'s in the tens place, it\'s 90, so 47×90? Wait, no. Wait the example says "Multiply number1 27 by the tens place digit 50 of number2 153". Wait in their example, the number2 is 153, so the tens digit is 5, so they multiplied by 50. So in our case, the tens digit is 9, so we should multiply by 9×10 = 90. But the way they presented it was "Multiply number1 27 by the tens place digit 50 of number2 153". So they converted the digit into its place value (50 instead of 5).\n\nSo following that structure:\n\nStep 1: Multiply 47 by 0 (the ones digit) → 47 × 0 = 0.\n\nStep 2: Multiply 47 by 90 (the tens place digit, which is 9, so 9×10=90). Wait, but actually, the tens digit is 9, so the value is 90. So multiplying 47 by 90. But that would be the entire step. However, in the example, they broke it down into multiplying by the digit and then the place. Let me check the example again.\n\nIn the example provided by the user:\n\nOriginal question was 27 × 153. The reasoning steps were:\n\n1. Multiply 27 by 3 (the ones place digit of 153).\n2. Multiply 27 by 50 (the tens place digit 5, so 50).\n3. Multiply 27 by 100 (the hundreds place digit 1, so 100).\n\nThen sum all three results.\n\nAh,