×79\n-------\nFirst multiply 31 by 9: 279\nThen multiply 31 by 70 (shift one position to the left):\n31 ×70 = 2170\nAdd them: 279 +2170 = 2449. \n\nYes, that seems correct. Let me verify using another method, like the distributive property or difference of squares. Alternatively, 31×80 would be 2480, so subtract 31 gives 2480-31=2449. Yep, that works.\n\nWait, but the initial problem in the user\'s example had 27 and 153, but the actual question here is 31 and 79. So the answer should be 2449. Let me make sure I didn\'t make any arithmetic errors here. Let me recompute:\n\n31 × 9: 9×30=270, 9×1=9 → 279. Correct.\n\n31 ×70: 31×7=217, so 217×10=2170. Correct.\n\n279 +2170: 2170 + 200 is 2370, plus 79 more: 2370+79=2449. Yep, that\'s correct.\n\nSo the final answer is 2449. Therefore, the reasoning steps should be written accordingly in the template.\n\nWait, but the user\'s original instruction had a template where they broke down 153 into 100 + 50 +3, so three steps. Since 79 is two digits, we do two steps. Let me structure the answer exactly as the example, but with the correct numbers here.\n\nThe example had:\n\n1. Multiply number1 (27) by the ones digit (3) → 27×3=81\n\n2. Multiply number1 by the tens digit (50) → 27×50=1350\n\n3. Multiply by hundreds digit (100) → 27×100=2700\n\nThen sum all: 81+1350+2700=4131.\n\nSimilarly, for 31 and 79 (which is two digits), the steps would be:\n\n1. Multiply 31 by 9 (ones place): 279\n\n2. Multiply 31 by 70 (tens place): 2170\n\nThen sum them: 279+2170=2449.\n\nSo the reasoning should follow that structure. I need to make sure the answer is presented in the template as specified.\n']
