9 and 75. Wait, let me confirm the question again.\n\nLooking back: The user provided:\n\n# Question:\nWhat is the product of <<number1 such as 27>> and <<number2 such as 153>>?\nThen in the actual problem, it's replaced with:\n\n# Question:\nWhat is the product of 59 and 75?\n\nAh, right, so the numbers are 59 and 75. I think I misread the problem earlier and thought of 17 and 97. That's my mistake. Let me start over with the correct numbers.\n\nSo, the actual question is 59 multiplied by 75. Let's do this step by step.\n\nFirst, breaking down 75 into 70 + 5. So multiply 59 by 70 and 59 by 5, then add the results.\n\nCalculating 59 × 70:\n\n59 × 70 is the same as 59 × 7 × 10. Let's compute 59 ×7 first.\n\n59 ×7: 50×7=350, 9×7=63. So 350+63=413. Then multiply by 10: 4130.\n\nNext, 59 ×5: 50×5=250, 9×5=45. So 250+45=295.\n\nNow add 4130 +295.\n\n4130 + 200 is 4330, plus 95 is 4425. So total is 4425.\n\nAlternatively, using the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n   59\nx 75\n-----\nFirst multiply 59 by 5: 295.\n\nThen multiply 59 by 70 (shift one position to the left):\n\n59 ×70 = 4130.\n\nAdding 295 +4130 gives 4425. So that's correct.\n\nWait, but let me check again in case of any arithmetic errors. Let me verify 59×75 another way, perhaps using the distributive property differently.\n\nAlternatively, 59×75 can be written as (60 -1)×75 = 60×75 -1×75.\n\n60×75 is 4500, and 1×75 is 75. So 4500 -75 = 4425. Yep, same result.\n\nTherefore, the answer should be 4425.\n\nWait, but in my initial wrong problem with 17×97, I got confused. But now, focusing on 59×75, all methods give 4425. So that's correct.\n\nI think that's solid. Let me structure the reasoning as per the template.\n\nThe user's example had:\n\nReasoning:\n\nLet's think step by step. 153 has three digits, so three steps.\n\n1. Multiply 27 by 3 (ones place): 81\n\n2. Multiply 27 by 50 (tens place): 1350\n\n3. Multiply 27 by 100 (hundreds place): 2700\n\nSum: 81 +1350=1431; 1431+2700=4131\n\nSo answer is 41